gs-native-pc (patch 69): one depth buffer per ZBP

A RenderTarget owned one depth texture and attached it for whatever ZBP the draw used. ZBP is an address
in VRAM, so two ZBPs are two different depth regions; reusing one texture means the second buffer starts
with the first one's contents (and, with the game's Z-inversion shadow passes, with the wrong sign too).

Measured first: zbpchg in GOW_GS_HW_STATS counts re-attachments, ~2000 per 5 s at 0.5x, so this is a live
path, not a theoretical one. The depth-texture line then shows the real scope: exactly one target in the
opening level (fbp=348) alternates between zbp 68 and 15c, every frame.

Implementation: RenderTarget keeps `depthByZbp` (zbp -> GL texture). Switching to a ZBP whose texture
already exists only re-attaches it with FramebufferTexture2D - no clear, no allocation - and a new ZBP
creates one more. Capped at four per target (kDepthsPerTarget); past that the attached texture is reused
and `zbpfull` counts it, because unbounded growth is worse than a wrong depth. destroy() deletes the map.

Counts with the fix on / off, same 25 s run (the number of "[gs-hw] depth" lines is the evidence, since a
reused texture prints nothing):
  on  : 5 depth textures, fbp=348 holds zbp 68 *and* 15c
  off : 4 depth textures (GOW_GS_DEPTH_PER_ZBP=0 restores the old behaviour)
Frame distribution unchanged within the scene-drift noise (26.2 vs 23.8 guest fps means, 25 s each), which
is expected: one extra 32x256 depth texture and one re-attach per switch.

Not verified: pixels. The Z-sampling paths (findReinterpretSource, the shuffle source) read the *attached*
depth for `t.zbp`, so a ZBP that has a texture but is not attached is now not matched at all - before it
was matched with another buffer's contents. Missing is the safer of the two, but it needs a frame
comparison against a recording to call it done.

